Each box performs a specific job.
Let's study them one by one.
________________________________________
Step 1 – You Enter a Website Address
Suppose you type:
https://www.google.com
This is called a URL.
________________________________________
Definition
URL (Uniform Resource Locator)
A URL is the complete address of a resource on the Internet.
Example:
https://www.google.com/search?q=networking
A URL tells your browser:
•	Which protocol to use (HTTPS)
•	Which server to contact (www.google.com)
•	Which resource to request (/search)
•	Additional information (the query)
________________________________________
Analogy
Imagine ordering food.
You must provide:
•	Restaurant
•	Address
•	Order
A URL is like the complete delivery address.
________________________________________
Step 2 – The Browser Receives Your Request
The browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, etc.) begins preparing the request.
The browser asks:
"I know the website's name...
But where is it located?"
The browser cannot send information using names.
Computers communicate using numbers.
________________________________________
Why?
Humans remember:
google.com
Computers prefer:
142.250.190.78
These numerical addresses are called IP addresses, which we'll explore in depth in the next chapter.
________________________________________
Step 3 – Browser Cache
Before asking anyone else, the browser checks its memory.
This temporary storage is called the browser cache.
If you've visited the website recently, your browser may already know its IP address.
This saves time.
________________________________________
Why Caching Exists
Imagine asking someone for your friend's phone number every day.
Eventually, you would memorize it.
Computers do the same.
Caching reduces unnecessary work and speeds up browsing.
________________________________________
Step 4 – Operating System Cache
If the browser doesn't know the answer, it asks the operating system.
Windows, Linux, and macOS also remember recently used addresses.
Again, this speeds up communication.
________________________________________
Step 5 – DNS
If neither the browser nor the operating system knows the IP address, the computer asks a DNS server.
DNS stands for:
Domain Name System
DNS is often called the phonebook of the Internet.
________________________________________
Definition
DNS
A distributed system that translates human-readable names into IP addresses.
Example:
google.com
↓
142.250.xxx.xxx
Without DNS, every website would need to be remembered by its numerical IP address.
________________________________________
Analogy
Imagine looking up a person's name in your phone contacts.
You remember:
Alice
Your phone remembers:
0917-123-4567
DNS performs the same translation for computers.
________________________________________
Real-World Example
You type:
youtube.com
DNS replies:
142.xxx.xxx.xxx
Now your computer finally knows where to send the request.
________________________________________
Why DNS Is Important
Without DNS:
•	Every website would require memorizing numbers.
•	The Internet would be much harder to use.
•	Businesses couldn't easily change server IP addresses without informing every user.
________________________________________
Step 6 – Creating the Request
Now your computer prepares a message.
This message contains information such as:
•	Destination IP address
•	Your IP address
•	Requested webpage
•	Security information (for HTTPS)
•	Browser details
Think of it as placing a letter inside an envelope with both the sender's and recipient's addresses.
________________________________________
Data Is Split into Packets
Large messages are not sent as one huge block.
Instead, they are divided into many smaller pieces called packets.
Example:
Large File
↓
Packet 1
Packet 2
Packet 3
Packet 4
Packet 5
...
Each packet travels independently and is reassembled at the destination.
This improves speed and reliability.
________________________________________
Why Packets?
Imagine moving an entire house.
Would you move everything in one trip?
No.
You would pack items into many boxes.
If one box is delayed, the others can still arrive.
Networks work the same way.
________________________________________
Key Takeaways
•	A URL is a human-friendly address.
•	Computers communicate using IP addresses.
•	Browsers and operating systems use caches to speed up browsing.
•	DNS translates names into IP addresses.
•	Data is divided into packets before transmission.
________________________________________
Memory Tips
URL
The address you type.
DNS
The Internet's phonebook.
Cache
Temporary memory used to speed things up.
Packet
A small piece of data sent across a network.
