Networking Fundamentals: From Zero to Professional Network Engineer
Chapter 2 – How the Internet Really Works
Part 2 – The Journey Across the Internet
________________________________________
Learning Objectives
By the end of this part, you will be able to:
•	Explain how data leaves your computer.
•	Understand the role of the Network Interface Card (NIC).
•	Describe how switches, routers, and Internet Service Providers (ISPs) forward packets.
•	Explain why packets may take different routes.
•	Understand the concept of routing and redundancy.
________________________________________
Review of Part 1
In Part 1, you learned that:
1.	You typed a URL.
2.	The browser checked its cache.
3.	The operating system checked its cache.
4.	DNS translated the domain name into an IP address.
5.	Your computer prepared the request.
6.	The request was divided into packets.
Now we will follow those packets as they travel across the Internet.
________________________________________
The Journey Begins
Imagine your packet is a traveler.
It has:
•	A destination (Google's server)
•	A return address (your computer)
•	Instructions on where it needs to go
Its job is simple:
Reach the destination as efficiently and reliably as possible.
________________________________________
Step 7 – The Network Interface Card (NIC)
Before your computer can send anything, it needs hardware that can communicate with a network.
This hardware is called the Network Interface Card (NIC).
Definition
A Network Interface Card (NIC) is a hardware component that connects a device to a network.
Examples:
•	Ethernet port
•	Wi-Fi adapter
•	USB Wi-Fi dongle
•	Built-in wireless card on a laptop
Without a NIC, a computer cannot communicate with other devices.
________________________________________
Real-World Example
When you connect your laptop to Wi-Fi:
Laptop
→ Wi-Fi Adapter (NIC)
→ Router
When using an Ethernet cable:
Laptop
→ Ethernet NIC
→ Switch or Router
________________________________________
Why Is a NIC Important?
The NIC:
•	Sends data.
•	Receives data.
•	Converts digital information into signals that can travel through cables or wireless radio waves.
•	Has its own unique identifier called a MAC Address (covered in a later chapter).
________________________________________
Step 8 – Data Leaves Your Computer
Your NIC transmits packets to the next device.
Depending on your connection, this may be:
•	A Wi-Fi Access Point
•	A Home Router
•	A Network Switch
Think of the NIC as the front door of your house.
Every package entering or leaving must pass through it.
________________________________________
Step 9 – The Home Router
Your home router is one of the most important devices in your network.
Its main job is to connect:
Your Home Network ↔ The Internet
A router makes forwarding decisions.
It examines the destination IP address and determines the next hop.
________________________________________
Analogy
Imagine driving to another city.
At each major intersection, road signs tell you which direction to go.
Routers work similarly.
They don't know every street in the world, but they know where to send traffic next.
________________________________________
Router vs. Switch
Switch	Router
Connects devices within the same local network	Connects different networks
Uses MAC addresses	Uses IP addresses
Operates inside a LAN	Connects LANs to WANs
________________________________________
Step 10 – Your Internet Service Provider (ISP)
After leaving your router, packets reach your ISP.
Examples of ISPs include companies that provide your home Internet connection.
The ISP owns large networking equipment that forwards traffic toward its destination.
Without an ISP, your home network would remain isolated from the global Internet.
________________________________________
The Internet Is Not One Giant Network
Many beginners imagine the Internet as one enormous network.
In reality, it is a network of networks.
Thousands of organizations connect their own networks together.
These include:
•	ISPs
•	Universities
•	Governments
•	Cloud providers
•	Businesses
•	Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)
Together, they form the Internet.
________________________________________
Step 11 – Routers Across the Internet
Your packet doesn't travel directly to Google.
Instead, it passes through many routers.
Example:
Your Laptop
      │
      ▼
Home Router
      │
      ▼
ISP Router
      │
      ▼
Regional Router
      │
      ▼
National Backbone
      │
      ▼
International Backbone
      │
      ▼
Google Network
      │
      ▼
Google Server
Each router makes a forwarding decision.
This usually takes only milliseconds.
________________________________________
What Is Routing?
Routing is the process of selecting a path for data to travel across networks.
Routers compare available paths and choose where to forward packets.
Later in this course, you'll study routing protocols such as:
•	RIP
•	OSPF
•	EIGRP
•	BGP
These protocols help routers share information about the best available paths.
________________________________________
Why Don't Packets Always Follow the Same Route?
Imagine two roads leading to the same destination.
One road has heavy traffic.
The other is clear.
GPS may choose the faster road.
Internet routing works similarly.
Packets may travel along different paths because of:
•	Congestion
•	Equipment failures
•	Maintenance
•	Load balancing
•	Policy decisions
This makes the Internet resilient.
________________________________________
Redundancy
Suppose a fiber-optic cable is accidentally cut.
Does the entire Internet stop working?
Usually not.
Routers detect unavailable paths and send traffic through alternative routes.
This is called redundancy.
Redundancy is a key reason why the Internet remains available despite hardware failures.
________________________________________
Real-World Example
A construction crew accidentally damages an underground fiber cable.
Traffic that previously used that cable is automatically redirected through another route.
Users may notice a slight delay, but communication continues.
________________________________________
Practical Application
As a Network Security Engineer, understanding packet paths helps you:
•	Diagnose connectivity issues.
•	Identify routing problems.
•	Design resilient networks.
•	Investigate suspicious traffic.
•	Configure secure network architectures.
________________________________________
